> One problem I see with this approach, though, is that object
> detection displays would have to immediately be changed to '1's
> after the initial display, because they *could* have been moved
> right away if they are out of sight.  But you could always still
> use the '/' look to see what they were indicated as.  The '/'
> interface would have to be made less painless, too.

It's not painful enough for you?  ;-)

Seriously though, I like the idea of putting an as-yet-unused symbol
there to mean "unknown".  How about this: when you see an object, and
then leave the room, the symbol of the object stays there; but when
you see that same object again elsewhere, *then* it's replaced
with the "unknown" symbol.  I.e. only one extra symbol, and you can still
visually glance at objects where you last knew them to be; and you
know that it's not guaranteed to still be there, but at least it's
more accurate than before.

Another possibility is, as you said, to mark each object as it goes
out of sight; but instead of overwriting it with a standard symbol,
grey it out, or put in low intensity.  (Of course this requires certain
terminal/window capabilities.)

